<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William of Rubruck</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1220–c. 1293</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William of Rubruck was a Flemish Franciscan friar who travelled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> in 1253–1255 on behalf of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>. He reached the court of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Great Khan Möngke</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a>, where he took part in a religious debate, and wrote a detailed report on Mongol society, religion and politics, one of the most valuable medieval European accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…securing the overland routes under the Pax Mongolica, it allowed goods, ideas and people to travel between China, the Middle East and Europe, from merchants such as Marco Polo to envoys such as the Franciscan <b>William of Rubruck</b>, and fostered exchanges in astronomy, medicine, cartography and gunpowder technology.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Mongol rulers exempted the clergy of many religions from taxation, and in 1254 the Franciscan <b>William of Rubruck</b> took part in a debate between Christians, Muslims and Buddhists at the court of Möngke in Karakorum.</blockquote><a href="/government/nomadic-confederation">Nomadic confederation · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>None of the Europeans who visited China or Mongolia in the 13th and 14th centuries, among them <b>William of Rubruck</b> and Marco Polo, mentioned the wall.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
